<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Sayyid Ali Hamadani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1314">1314</a>–<a href="/years/1384">1384</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Shah-i-Hamadan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Sayyid Ali Hamadani was a Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> of the Kubrawiya order who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> in the 1370s and 1380s with many followers. He is credited with spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the valley and with introducing Persian crafts such as shawl-weaving. The Khanqah-e-Moula shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srinagar">Srinagar</a> commemorates him, and Kashmiris call him Shah-i-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-sayyid-ali-hamadani.jpg" alt="Mir Sayyid Ali Hamadani" width="194" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balti sahib · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MirSyedAliHamdani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
